          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em formal deformation quantization
            (often taken to be the default notion of
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            to a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbatively
            in small (really:
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
          %p
            Formal deformation quantization focuses on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            of a physical system (hence on the
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"} deform
            the commutative algebra of classical observables to a non-commutative algebra of quantum observables. (This is in contrast to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of states
            and hence on the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
          %p
            Usually and traditionally,
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            refers to (just)
            %em formal
            deformations, in the sense that it produces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            expansions in a formal parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            (physically:
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+approximation"} semiclassical approximation
                %th …
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℏ
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(\hbar^0)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℏ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(\hbar^1)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℏ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(\hbar^n)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℏ
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(\hbar^\infty)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
          %p
            (This is related to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
          %p
            But there are refinements of this to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebraic+deformation+quantization"} C-star algebraic deformation quantization
            which studies the proper deformation to a genuine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            of observables. (This in turn is related to genuine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            via the notion of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
          %p
            One can, therefore, argue that only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebraic+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            is genuine
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#GukovWitten09"} Gukov-Witten 09, section 1.4
          %blockquote
            %p
              Generally speaking,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
              is based on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              strict
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              quantization, rather than
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              formal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              deformation quantization, although conventional quantization sometimes leads to problems that can be treated by deformation quantization.
          %p
            As other methods of quantization, deformation quantization has as input a description of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            on the Poisson manifold with the same
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative unital product
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutator
            agrees, up to order
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            of algebras. Gluing local solutions to the quantization problem furthermore involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            and specifically
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbes"} gerbes
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            If the result of deformation quantization is an algebra over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}[ [ \hbar ] ]
            of a formal parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            (thought of as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar \to 0
            reproduces the starting point of the deformation, then one speaks of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#FormalDeformationQuantization"} Formal deformation quantization
          %p
            In much of the literature this is regarded as the default meaning of “deformation quantization”. But this is really the case corresponding to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#StrictDeformationQuantization"} Strict deformation quantization
          %p
            is supposed to result in a non-formal deformation, which in terms of the above formal power series at least means that one can set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar = 1
            such that all expressions in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            converge, but which in general is taken to mean something stronger, such as that there is a continuous field of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebraic+deformation+quantization"} C-star algebraic deformation quantization
          %h3#FormalDeformationQuantization Formal deformation quantization
          %p We first give the traditional
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#ExplicitDefinitionOfTraditionalDeformationQuantization"} Explicit definition of traditional deformation quantization
          %p
            of a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            this describes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} system
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            More abstractly, this may be formulated and generalized in terms of lifts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+over+an+operad"} algebras over an operad
            over a
            %span.newWikiWord
              P-n operad
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/P-n+operad"} ?
            to a
            %span.newWikiWord
              BD-n operad
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/BD-n+operad"} ?
            and hence an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-n+operad"} E-n operad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#FormulationAsLiftsFromPnAlgebrasToBDAlgebras"} Formulation as lifts from P-n algebras to BD-n algebras
          %p
            In this formulation one sees that for genral
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            the construction applies to
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            be 1-dimensional quantum field theory, for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            “on the
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local
            quantum field theory formulated in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebras+of+observables"} factorization algebras of observables
            over
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            is indicated in (
            %a{:href => "#CostelloGwilliam"}> Costello-Gwilliam, chapter 5
            ).
          %h4#ExplicitDefinitionOfTraditionalDeformationQuantization Explicit definition of deformation of Poisson manifolds/Poisson algebras
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = C^\infty(M)
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
            of smooth functions.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
                \-product
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"}> star product
              ) on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a product on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A [ [ t ] ]
              that is (1) bilinear over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}[ [ t ] ]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in A
              it can be written out as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \ast b = \sum_{n=0}^\infty B_n(a,b) t^n
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_n
              are bilinear maps on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi ab
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_0(a,b) = ab
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A (formal)
              %strong deformation quantization
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = C^\infty(M)
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b\} = B_1(a,b) - B_1(b,a)
              for
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}[ [ t ] ]
          %h4#FormulationAsLiftsFromPnAlgebrasToBDAlgebras
            Formulation as lifts from
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
