          %h4#InPerturbativeQuantumFieldTheory Deformation quantization in perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            Deformation quantization in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            is discussed for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+deformation+quantization"} Moyal deformation quantization
            yielding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            in (
            %a{:href => "#DuetschFredenhagen00"}> Dütsch-Fredenhagen 00
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#HirschfeldHenselder02"} Hirschfeld-Henselder 02
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"}> perturbative AQFT
            ) via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov deformation quantization
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Collini16"}> Collini 16
            ), see also (
            %a{:href => "#HawkinsRejzner16"}> Hawkins-Rejzner 16
            ).
          %h3#StrictDeformationQuantization Strict deformation quantization
          %p
            (…)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebraic+deformation+quantization"} C-star algebraic deformation quantization
            (…)
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#existence_results Existence results
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Drinfel%27d"} Vladimir Drinfel'd
            has sketched a proof (and gave main ingredients) to show that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+group"} Poisson Lie group
            can be deformation quantized to a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            proved a certain
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kontsevich+formality"} formality theorem
            (formality is here in the sense of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dg-algebra"} formal dg-algebra
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            has a deformation quantization (
            %a{:href => "#Kontsevich97"}> Kontsevich 97
            ).
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            and those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            that have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+foliation"} regular foliation
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+leafs"} symplectic leafs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boris+Fedosov"} Boris Fedosov
            gave a construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+products"} star products
            on symplectic manifolds using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+connections"} symplectic connections
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            (
            %a{:href => "#Fedosov94"}> Fedosov 94
            ), see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov%27s+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov's deformation quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roman+Bezrukavnikov"} Roman Bezrukavnikov
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Kaledin"} Dmitry Kaledin
            in the context of an algebraic symplectic form (
            %a{:href => "#BK"}> BK 04
            ).
          .standout
            %p
              Caution: the following are rough notes from a talk by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.D.S.+Jones"} J.D.S. Jones
              (Cambridge, 8.1.2013); there are probably many typos and sign errors.
          %h4#deformation_of_poisson_manifolds Deformation of Poisson manifolds
          #Kontsevich.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong> (Kontsevich)
              \. Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
              has a (formal) deformation quantization.
          %p
            This was shown in (
            %a{:href => "#Kontsevich97"}> Kontsevich 97
            ). There the deformed product is constructed by a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            for the given Poisson manifold. See there for more details.
          %h4#deformation_of_algebraic_varieties Deformation of Algebraic Varieties
          %p (Not from the notes of J.D.S. Jones)
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a smooth algebraic variety over a field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{k}
            of characteristic
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          #SwYe.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (Swan,
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.4153/CJM-2002-051-8"} Yekutieli
              There is a canonical isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Ext
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                      %mi q
                    %msup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} H
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\operatorname{Ext}^i_{X^2}(\mathcal{O}_X, \mathcal{O}_X) \cong
                    \\bigoplus_q \operatorname{H}^{i - q}(X, \bigwedge^q (\mathcal{T}_X)) .
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_X
            is the tangent sheaf of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is embedded diagonally in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^2
          %p
            This is a consequence of the following result. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mi cd
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{cd, X}
            be the sheaf of continuous Hochschild cochains of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X^2}
          #Yek1.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.4153/CJM-2002-051-8"}> Yekutieli
                ).
            %ol
              %li
                %p There is a canonical isomorphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                        %msub
                          %mi ℋℴ𝓂
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi X
                              %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mrow
                            %mi cd
                            %mo ,
                            %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\operatorname{R} \mathcal{Hom}_{X^2}(\mathcal{O}_X, \mathcal{O}_X) \cong
                        \\mathcal{C}_{cd, X}
                %p
                  in the derived category of
                  = succeed "-modules." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒪
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi X
                                %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X^2}
              %li
                %p
                  There is a canonical quasi-isomorphism of complexes of
                  = succeed "-modules" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒪
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi X
                                %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X^2}
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                          %mi q
                        %mover
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                          %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mrow
                            %mi cd
                            %mo ,
                            %mi X
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigoplus_q \bigwedge^q (\mathcal{T}_X)[-q] \to \mathcal{C}_{cd, X}  .
              %li
                %p Therefore there is a canonical isomorphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                        %msub
                          %mi ℋℴ𝓂
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi X
                              %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                          %mi q
                        %mover
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                          %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\operatorname{R} \mathcal{Hom}_{X^2}(\mathcal{O}_X, \mathcal{O}_X) \cong
                        \\bigoplus_q \bigwedge^q (\mathcal{T}_X)[-q]
                %p
                  in the derived category of
                  = succeed "-modules." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒪
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi X
                                %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X^2}
          %p
            The relation to deformation quantization is this:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mi cd
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{cd, X}
            is a shift by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            of the sheaf of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mrow
                      %mi poly
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}_{poly, X}
            of polydifferential operators (viewed only as a complex of quasi-coherent
            = succeed "-modules)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X^2}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi q
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigoplus_q \bigwedge^q (\mathcal{T}_X)[-q]
            is the shift by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            of the sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mrow
                      %mi poly
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_{poly, X}
            of polyvector fields. Thus item 2 in the theorem above says that there is a canonical
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X^2}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mrow
                      %mi poly
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mrow
                      %mi poly
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_{poly, X} \to \mathcal{D}_{poly, X} .
          %p
            Trying to replicate the global formality theorem of Kontsevich, one would like to upgrade this to an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} L
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{L}_{\infty}
            quasi-isomorphism. However, it seems that in general this cannot be done directly, but only after a suitable resolution.
          %p
            Here is the result. (See also
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2007.02.012"} Van den Bergh
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            admits a canonical flasque resolution called the mixed resolution:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mix
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M} \to \operatorname{Mix}(\mathcal{M}) .
          %p
            This “mixes” the jet resolution with the Cech resolution (corresponding to an affine open covering of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that we suppress). In particular there are quasi-isomorphisms of sheaves of DG Lie algebras
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mrow
                      %mi poly
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mix
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mrow
                      %mi poly
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_{poly, X} \to \operatorname{Mix}(\mathcal{T}_{poly, X})
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mrow
                      %mi poly
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mix
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mrow
                      %mi poly
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}_{poly, X} \to \operatorname{Mix}(\mathcal{D}_{poly, X}) .
          #Yek2.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.bgu.ac.il/~amyekut/publications/def-quant/def-quant.html"}> Yekutieli
                ).
              There is an
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{L}_{\infty}
              quasi-isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mix
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mrow
                        %mi poly
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mix
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mrow
                        %mi poly
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Psi : \operatorname{Mix}(\mathcal{T}_{poly, X}) \to
                    \\operatorname{Mix}(\mathcal{D}_{poly, X})
            %p
              whose
              = succeed "-st" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            A Poisson deformation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_X
            is a sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            of Poisson
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{k}[[\hbar]]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕜
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{k} \otimes_{\mathbb{k}[[\hbar]]} \mathcal{A} \cong \mathcal{O}_X
            called an augmentation. Likewise an associative deformation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_X
            is a sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            of associative unital (but noncommutative)
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{k}[[\hbar]]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_X
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Yek2"} Theorem 4
            implies:
          #Yek3.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_5 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Yekutieli).
              Assume that the cohomology groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} H
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{H}^{1}(X, \mathcal{O}_X)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} H
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{H}^{2}(X, \mathcal{O}_X)
              vanish. Then there is a canonical bijection
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} quant
                    %mo :
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mtext Poisson deformations of
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mtext isomorphism
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ≃
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mtext associative deformations of
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mtext isomorphism
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathrm{quant} : \quad
                    \\frac{ \{ \text{ Poisson deformations of} \; \mathcal{O}_X \} }{\text{isomorphism}} \quad \xrightarrow{\, \simeq \,} \quad
                    \\frac{ \{ \text{ associative deformations of} \; \mathcal{O}_X \} }{\text{isomorphism}}
            %p called quantization. It preserves first order brackets.
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is affine this is Theorem 0.1 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.bgu.ac.il/~amyekut/publications/def-quant/def-quant.html"} this paper
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.bgu.ac.il/~amyekut/publications/twisted-defs/twisted-defs.html"} this paper
          %p
            For twisted (or stacky) deformations there is a corresponding (but much more difficult to state and prove). See the
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.bgu.ac.il/~amyekut/publications/twisted-defs/twisted-defs.html"} paper
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.bgu.ac.il/~amyekut/publications/tw-defs-surv/tw-defs-surv.html"} survey
          %h4#gerstenhabers_deformation_theory_by_hochschild_cohomology Gerstenhaber’s deformation theory by Hochschild (co)homology
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^p(V,V) = \Hom(V^{\otimes p}, V)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
            as follows: for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in C^p(V,V)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in C^q(V,V)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi q
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ g \in C^{p+q-1}(V,V)
            as the map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi q
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mi q
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi q
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f \circ g)(v_1, \ldots v_{p+q-1}) = f(v_1, \ldots, v_{i-1}, g(v_i, \ldots, v_{i+q-1}), v_{i+q}, \ldots, v_{p+q-1}).
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in C^\ast(V,V)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
