                      %mo =
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \infty
            %p
              Moreover, this is
              %em always
              the case for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#PenkavaVanhaecke00"}> Penkava-Vanhaecke 00, theorem 3.2 with section 2
            )
          #DeformationQuantizationOfLiePoissonStructuresByUniversalEnvelopingAlgebras.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Poisson+structures"} Lie-Poisson structures
                by universal enveloping algebras)
            %p
              In the following cases the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
              in prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UniversalEnvelopingAlgebraProvidesDeformationQuantizationAtLeastToOrder3"}
              is injective to arbitrary order, hence in these cases the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+enveloping+algebra"} universal enveloping algebra
              provides a genuine
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The case that the Poisson bracket is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+Poisson+structure"} linear
                  in that it restricts as
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi V
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi V
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi V
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi Sym
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\{-,-\}
                        \\;\colon\;
                        V \otimes V
                        \\longrightarrow
                        V
                        \\hookrightarrow
                        Sym(V)
                        \\,.
                %p
                  This is the case of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Poisson+structure"} Lie-Poisson structure
                  from example
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PolynomialLiePoissonStructure"}
                  and the universal enveloping algebra that provides its deformation quantization is the standard one (example
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UniversalEnvelopingAlgebraOfLieAlgebra"}
              %li
                %p More generally, the case that the Poisson bracket restricted to linear elements has linear and constant contribution in that it restricts as
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi V
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi V
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi V
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi Sym
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\{-,-\} \;\colon\; V \otimes V \longrightarrow \mathbb{R} \oplus V \hookrightarrow Sym(V)
                        \\,.
                %p
                  This includes notably the Poisson structures induced by
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+spaces"} symplectic vector spaces
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\{-,-\} \;\colon\; (\mathbb{R} \oplus V) \otimes (\mathbb{R} \oplus V) \longrightarrow (\mathbb{R} \oplus V)
                %p
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
                  of the associated
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+Lie+algebra"} Heisenberg Lie algebra
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#PenkavaVanhaecke00"} Penkava &amp; Vanhaecke 2000, p. 26
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Gutt11"} Gutt 2011
          %h3#MotivicGaloisGroup Motivic Galois group action on the space of quantizations
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "Kontsevich99"}> Kontsevich 99
            ) it was indicated that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+group"} quotient group
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"} motivic Galois group
            apparently equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck-Teichm%C3%BCller+group"} Grothendieck-Teichmüller group
            naturally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acts
            on the space of formal deformation quantizations of a finite dimensional manifold. See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+Galois+group"} cosmic Galois group
          %p
            This has been formalized as follows. The formal deformation quantization of (
            %a{:href => "#Kontsevich97"}> Kontsevich 97
            ) is all induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kontsevich+formality"} Kontsevich formality
            theorem, which states that over suitable
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/varieties"} varieties
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} L-∞ algebras
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒳
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{X}(X) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} C^\bullet(X)
          %p
            identifying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivector+fields"} multivector fields
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild cohomology
            complex (of its
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebra"} function algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Therefore one may regard the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Maps
                    %mi equiv
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒳
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Maps^{L_\infty}_{equiv}(\mathcal{X}(X), C^\bullet(X))
            as the “space of formal deformation quantizations” of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Dolgushev1109"}> Dolgushev 1109, theorem 6.2
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Dolgushev1111"} Dolgushev 1111, theorem 3.1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Maps
                    %mi equiv
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒳
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 Maps^{L_\infty}_{equiv}(\mathcal{X}(X), C^\bullet(X))
            of connected components of this space is, up to a choice of basepoint, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck-Teichm%C3%BCller+group"} Grothendieck-Teichmüller group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
            over that group.
          %p
            (This is based on identifications of the GRT Lie algebra with the degree-0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+cohomology"} chain cohomology
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+complex"} graph complex
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Willwacher"} Thomas Willwacher
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Grothendieck-Teichm%C3%BCller+tower#RelationToTheGraphComplex"} Grothendieck-Teichmüller group – relation to the graph complex
          %p
            Aspects of the generalization of this statement to more general spaces then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            are discussed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DolgushevRogersWillwacher12"} Dolgushev-Rogers-Willwacher 12
          %p
            For discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motive"} motivic structures
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
